      "id": "plate-to-space",
      "kind": "split",
      "seconds": 6.5,
      "caption": "The tile is flat. Open it and it stands in space.",
      "narration": "This one is the Preview engine: the picture itself, lifted onto a plane and turned.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "id": "gallery-assets",
          "url": "photo-time-machine-living-room-server-acme.ci.computer",
          "alt": "The Explore tab: the finished splat as a gallery tile, labelled Splat, Preview and its creation date.",
          "_focusNote": "Landscape only, and deliberately asymmetric with the other cell. A 16:9 split cell is 806px of a 3840px plate, so the uncropped desktop plate puts the Explore card — the thing the caption says is flat — at about a quarter of the cell width, under a hero headline that dominates it. Cropped to 0.36 the card fills the cell and 'porch-1998 / Splat · Preview · Jun 15, 2026' is readable, which is what makes the left half a TILE rather than a screenshot of a website. Checked by cropping the committed PNG to the cell and looking at it, per AUTHORING; not previewable with focus-preview, which reconstructs a camera move and a split has no camera.\n\nPortrait is left at the whole plate on purpose: the 9:16 cell is 475px of a 1080px plate and the mobile layout already presents the card at about 40% of the cell width with its label legible, and the portrait cell floor is 0.594 anyway — a crop tight enough to matter is not reachable there.\n\nThe other cell keeps the whole plate: it is a full-bleed 3D view and cropping it would only throw away the space the caption is pointing at.",
          "focus": {
            "landscape": {
              "x": 0.5,
              "y": 0.35,
              "w": 0.36,
              "h": 0.36
            }
